</code></pre> <p>The root node can generate multiple accounts, each with its own unique keys and address. This allows a single mnemonic to manage many accounts while keeping them cryptographically isolated.</p> <p>Deriving an account requires specifying an account index. 6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:99b01b221406ef2dd2bae1e2ab6fa8f0:&lt;__None__&gt;6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:1fc37bab7c6e07839ec567c0cd646b9b:&lt;__None__&gt;6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:9eaf22c87c7a325456830b6d91a84c39:&lt;__None__&gt; generates the derivation path (a standardized string that specifies which account to derive) for that index, and 6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:7de7da07d06a7be0c6b6567c10d71880:&lt;__None__&gt;6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:d7259cb2a52b434cbaf0f27e5e18089a:&lt;__None__&gt;6251a85a-47d0-11ee-be56-0242ac120002:87d71c6589ae437c8b741a71c449ad2c:&lt;__None__&gt; follows that path to create the account.</p> <p>In this example, the account at index <code>0</code> is derived. Additional accounts can be derived by using different indices (e.g., <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>3</code>, ...). Each index produces a completely different account.</p>

